course: dictionary
lesson: 7516
title: Pow: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word pow can be a sound, a short form, or a word for something sudden. Look at the words and pictures around it before you decide.
explain: In a comic, «Pow! Biff! Bang!» are sound words, so pow is the noise of a punch. In a history book, «a former POW» uses capital letters as a short form, and POW means a prisoner of war: a soldier captured and held by the enemy.
explain: Now read this: «And then, pow, the lights went out.» Nobody is punched here, so pow must show that something sudden and exciting happened. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have it.
fact: Pow can be a sound word: «Pow!» is the noise of a punch, a bang or a small explosion.
fact: In comics, sound words like «pow» and «biff» make the action feel loud.
fact: POW is an abbreviation for prisoner of war, a soldier captured and held by the enemy. POW camps held captured soldiers in the World Wars.
fact: Informal pow shows that something sudden and exciting happened, as in «And then, pow, the lights went out.»
fact: «Kuài pǎo» («KWIGH POW») means run! in Chinese, and it sounds a bit like pow. The word in the lesson sentence, «a pow-wow», means a meeting to talk things over.
exercise: In a comic, «Pow! Biff! Bang!» What does pow mean? | A kind of pow-wow | A type of bread | A prisoner of war | *The sound of a punch
exercise: «The old soldier was a former POW.» What does POW mean? | *A prisoner of war | A meeting to talk things over | A large bang in a comic | A powerful wizard
exercise: «And then, pow, the lights went out.» What does pow show here? | *Something sudden and exciting happened | A loud punch in a fight | A soldier being captured | A quiet, slow walk
test: «They held "a palaver, an indaba, a punchayet, and a pow-wow": four words from different lands.» What does pow-wow mean? | A sudden bang | A camp for captured soldiers | A punch in a fight | *A meeting to talk things over
test: «Kuài pǎo» («KWIGH POW») is Chinese. What does it mean? | Bubble | Meeting | *Run! | Cannon
exam: «The old soldier had been a POW in a camp for two years.» What does POW mean here? | *A soldier captured and held by the enemy | A sudden, exciting surprise | A loud bang in a comic | A meeting to talk things over
